
wildcards are special characters used by the shell to match filenames and paths. They are also called globbing patterns.



| Character | Meaning                                      | Example                          | What it matches                                 |
| --------- | -------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| `*`       | Zero or more characters                      | `ls *.txt`                       | `a.txt`, `file.txt`, `backup.txt`               |
| `?`       | Exactly one character                        | `ls file?.txt`                   | `file1.txt`, `fileA.txt`                        |
| `[abc]`   | One character from the set                   | `ls file[abc].txt`               | `filea.txt`, `fileb.txt`, `filec.txt`           |
| `[a-z]`   | Any lowercase letter                         | `ls file[a-z].txt`               | `filea.txt` – `filez.txt`                       |
| `[A-Z]`   | Any uppercase letter                         | `ls file[A-Z].txt`               | `fileA.txt` – `fileZ.txt`                       |
| `[0-9]`   | Any digit                                    | `ls file[0-9].txt`               | `file0.txt` – `file9.txt`                       |
| `[!abc]`  | Any character except `a`, `b`, `c` *(shell)* | `ls file[!abc].txt`              | `filed.txt`, `file1.txt`                        |
| `[^abc]`  | Any character except `a`, `b`, `c` *(regex)* | `grep '[^abc]' file`             | Lines containing a character other than `a/b/c` |
| `\`       | Escape character                             | `ls my\ file.txt`                | File named `my file.txt`                        |
| `^`       | Beginning of line *(regex)*                  | `grep '^root' file`              | `root:x:0:0...`                                 |
| `$`       | End of line *(regex)*                        | `grep 'bash$' file`              | Lines ending in `bash`                          |
| `.`       | Any single character *(regex)*               | `grep 'c.t' file`                | `cat`, `cut`, `cot`                             |
| `+`       | One or more occurrences *(regex)*            | `grep -E 'ab+' file`             | `ab`, `abb`, `abbb`                             |
| `\|`      | OR *(regex)*                                 | `grep -E 'error\|failed' file`   | Lines containing `error` or `failed`            |
| `()`      | Groups patterns *(regex)*                    | `grep -E '(error\|failed)' file` | `error` or `failed`                             |





*        → zero or more characters
?        → exactly one character
[abc]    → a OR b OR c
[a-z]    → any lowercase letter
[A-Z]    → any uppercase letter
[0-9]    → any digit
[!abc]   → anything except a, b, or c (shell)
[^abc]   → anything except a, b, or c (regex)
\        → escape character
^        → beginning of the line
$        → ending of the line
.        → any single character (regex)
+        → one or more occurrences (regex)
|        → OR (regex)
()       → grouping (regex)


